function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><h2>component</h2><div class=\"doc-section signatures\"><h3>Capy</h3><pre>function component(name : string) string\nfunction component(name : string, props : as dval) string</pre></div><div class=\"doc-section content\"><h3>Description</h3><p><code>component()</code> renders a component and returns everything it printed as a string.</p><p>It collects the output into a buffer, which costs something. Reach for <code>component_render()</code> instead when you are writing the fragment straight into the current response and never look at the string — it skips the buffer entirely, so <code>component_render(&quot;card&quot;)</code> does less work than <code>print(component(&quot;card&quot;))</code>.</p><p>Capture the string when the string is the goal: wrapping the fragment in surrounding markup, checking whether it produced anything before emitting a container, storing it in a <code>dval</code> beside other values, or emitting the same fragment more than once from one call.</p><h2>How a name resolves</h2><p>A name is a path with an optional <code>:HANDLER</code> suffix. Bearer appends <code>.capy</code> when the path has no suffix, so <code>&quot;card&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;card.capy&quot;</code> mean the same file.</p><p>A path that starts with <code>/</code> is tried first as a real filesystem path and then as a site-root path, so <code>&quot;/tests/capy-request-context.capy&quot;</code> means that file under the site root.</p><p>A relative path is tried against three directories, in order:</p><ol><li><p>the directory of the entry unit that is handling the request</p></li><li><p>the directory of the unit calling <code>component()</code>, when that differs</p></li><li><p>the site root</p></li></ol><p>T")
    print("he first match wins. A resolved path that escapes the site root is rejected. Use <code>component_resolve()</code> to see which file a name reached. Test an optional name with <code>length(component_resolve(name)) &gt; 0</code> before you render it.</p><h2>Named handlers</h2><p><code>&quot;lib/widgets.capy:CARD&quot;</code> calls the <code>COMPONENT:CARD</code> handler in that file. A name that is only <code>&quot;:CARD&quot;</code> calls <code>COMPONENT:CARD</code> in the file that is currently rendering, which lets one file hold several related fragments.</p><p>A file with no named handler must define a plain <code>COMPONENT</code> handler.</p><h2>What the component sees</h2><p><code>props</code> arrives as <code>request.props</code>. It is swapped in for the duration of the call and restored when the component returns, so a component cannot disturb its caller&#39;s props, and nested components each read their own.</p><p>Relative paths inside the component resolve against the component&#39;s own file, not the caller&#39;s — a component in <code>lib/</code> can call <code>component(&quot;sibling&quot;)</code> and reach <code>lib/sibling.capy</code>.</p><p>The target unit&#39;s <code>ONCE</code> handler runs before the component handler the first time that unit is used in the request.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section params\"><h3>Parameters</h3><div><p>name : a component path, optionally followed by <code>:HANDLER</code></p></div><div><p>props : optional input, readable inside the component as <code>request.props</code></p></div><div></div></div><div class=\"doc-section\"><h3>Return Values</h3><p>The component output as a string.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section\"><h3>Errors</h3><p>An unresolvable component does not raise an error. Bearer sets the response status to <code>50")
    print("0</code>, returns a banner element that describes the failure, and continues to render the page. Guard optional components with <code>length(component_resolve(name)) &gt; 0</code> when a missing file should not be an error.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">print(component(&quot;/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD&quot;, {title: &quot;Release notes&quot;}), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">var body := component(&quot;/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD&quot;, {title: &quot;Ada&quot;})\nprint(&quot;&lt;section&gt;&quot;, body, &quot;&lt;/section&gt;&quot;, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">var titles := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\nfor title := titles {\n    print(component(&quot;/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD&quot;, {title: title}))\n}\nprint(&quot;\\n&quot;)\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">if length(component_resolve(&quot;/doc/examples/card.capy&quot;)) &gt; 0 {\n    print(component(&quot;/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD&quot;, {title: &quot;Optional&quot;}), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n} else {\n    print(&quot;no card component\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/component-render/\">component_render</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/component-resolve/\">component_resolve</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/handler/component/\">component</a><a cla")
    print("ss=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/request/\">request</a></aside></div>")
}
